WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.800
Two neutron stars caught in a death spiral.

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:11.360
This massive kilonova explosion not only
sheds light on the creation of heavy elements

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:18.140
such as gold and platinum, it also provides
scientists with a unique insight into one of

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:21.110
the most mysterious objects in the universe.

00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:25.730
Trying to imagine what a neutron star is
really like really challenges our

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:28.540
imagination. It also challenges our
theoretical physics.

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:32.420
We have to go to our computer models, our
mathematics, to have some estimate of what

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:33.470
this might be like.

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:37.780
Now, scientists don't have to rely on their
imaginations.

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:43.330
They can use hard data from the kilonova.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.400
To work out what makes neutron stars tick.

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:55.580
There's so much information we got from
observing that one single event, that one

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:57.170
colliding neutron star pair.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:01.430
Now, for the first time, we have an accurate
estimate of the mass of the neutron star and

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:05.720
the diameter. We can finally begin to piece
together how neutron stars really work.

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:10.490
They calculate the diameter is just 12.4
miles.

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:15.080
One mile less than the length of Manhattan.

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:20.350
Nailing down any physical characteristic is
really important.

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:24.580
And if there's going to be one, the radius
is a big one, because from there, if you know

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:25.980
the mass, you can get the density.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:29.830
And if you know the overall density, you can
start to figure out what the layering inside

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:31.300
of a neutron star is like.

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:41.750
For physicists, the interior of a neutron
star is one of the most intriguing places in

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:44.300
the universe. You have to realize that the.

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:48.020
Conditions inside a neutron star are very,
very different than the conditions that exist

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:53.330
here on Earth. We're talking about material
that's so dense that even the nuclei of atoms

00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:54.380
can't hold together.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:59.360
With the neutron star. You're taking
something that weighs more than the sun and

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:02.030
compressing it down to be smaller than a
city.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:07.010
It's so dense that if you tried to put it on
the ground, it would fall right through the

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:07.520
earth.

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:12.440
High density means high gravity.

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:15.980
Gravity 200 billion times greater than on
Earth.

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:20.750
Magic climbing up on a table in the surface
of a neutron star and jumping off.

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:25.430
You're going to just get flattened instantly
and just spread out on that surface.

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:27.710
So don't even think about trying to do push
ups.

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:32.610
Added to the intense gravity are hugely
powerful.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:34.800
Magnetic fields.

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:37.170
Awesome x ray radiation.

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:38.490
Electric fields.

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:44.490
30 million times more powerful than
lightning bolts and blizzards of high energy

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:50.790
particles. For a space traveler, this is not
a good neighborhood.

00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:59.720
If you were to find yourself in the vicinity
of a neutron star, it's going to be bad news.

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:04.500
First, you would be torn apart by the
incredibly strong magnetic fields.

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:11.600
Then the x ray radiation would blast you to
a crisp, and as it pulled you closer, its

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:17.100
intense gravity would stretch out your atoms
and molecules into a long, thin stream.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:21.890
You would build your speed faster and
faster, and you would finally impact the

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:24.920
surface splatter across it.

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:29.090
And that process would release as much
energy as a nuclear bomb.

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:37.500
If I had the choice between falling into a
neutron star versus a black hole, I think I'd

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:41.490
pick the black hole because I don't really
feel like being torn apart by a magnetic

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:43.350
field and blasted with x rays.

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:49.550
On a cosmic scale.

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:54.740
Neutron stars may be pint sized, but they
sure pack a serious punch.

00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:59.810
The secret to all this pent up power is
what's going on below the surface.

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:08.330
Armed with the new Kilonova data, we can now
take a virtual journey into the heart of a

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:09.560
neutron star.

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:12.770
First, we must pass through its atmosphere.

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:17.270
Now, it's not like the Earth's atmosphere,
which goes up like 100 miles on a neutron

00:04:17.270 --> 00:04:22.700
star. The atmosphere is about this deep, and
it's extremely dense compared to the air

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:23.540
around us.

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:31.310
Below the compressed atmosphere is a crust of
ionized iron, a mixture of crystal iron

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:34.940
nuclei and free flowing iron electrons.

00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:39.800
Now the gravity is so strong that it's almost
perfectly smooth.

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:42.920
The biggest mountains on the surface are
going to be less than a quarter of an inch

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:43.340
high.

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:52.540
A quarter inch mountain range may sound odd,
but things get even stranger as we go below

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:59.440
the surface. This is home to the strongest
material in the universe.

00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:04.140
It's so weird.

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:07.110
Scientists liken it to nuclear pasta.

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:14.150
As we dive beneath the crust of a neutron
star, the neutrons themselves start to glue

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:16.900
themselves together into exotic shapes.

00:05:16.910 --> 00:05:22.100
First, they form clumps that look something
like gnocchi.

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:28.340
Then deeper, the gnocchi glue themselves
together to form long strands that look like

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:35.120
spaghetti. Even deeper, the spaghetti fused
together to form sheets of lasagna.

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:41.180
And then finally, the lasagna fused together
to become a uniform mass, but with holes in

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:44.570
it. So it looks like penne.

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:46.820
This is pasta.

00:05:46.850 --> 00:05:52.430
Nuclear style simmering at a temperature of
over 1,000,000°F.

00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:59.690
Extreme gravity bends, squeezes, stretches
and buckles, neutrons creating a material

00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:04.250
100,000 billion times denser than iron.

00:06:04.370 --> 00:06:07.310
But the journey gets even more extreme.

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:11.010
Even deeper is more mysterious and harder to
understand.

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:17.280
The core of a neutron star, which is very
far away from these layers, which we call the

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:18.410
nuclear pasta.

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:21.840
It's perhaps the most exotic form of matter.

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:28.890
So exotic it might be the last bastion of
matter before complete gravitational collapse

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:30.150
into a black hole.

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:39.210
Data from NASA's Chandra Observatory suggests
the core is made up of a superfluid, a

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:42.300
bizarre, friction free state of matter.

00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:45.150
Superfluids produced in the lab exhibit.

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:52.770
Strange properties such as the ability to
flow upwards and escape airtight containers.

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:58.830
Although our knowledge of the stars interior
is still hazy, there's no mystery about its

00:06:58.830 --> 00:07:00.000
dazzling birth.

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:06.180
Forged into life during the most spectacular
event, the universe has to offer the

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:08.940
explosive death of a massive star.

00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:14.380
Neutron stars spark into life amid the death
of their parent star.

00:07:14.410 --> 00:07:18.880
They are the ultimate story of resurrection
or of life from death.

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:22.640
It's all part of a cosmic cycle.

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:27.560
Stars are born from giant clouds of very cold
gas.

00:07:28.220 --> 00:07:30.380
Those clouds collapse under their own
gravity.

00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:35.780
And the density of the core at the center of
that collapse starts to increase.

00:07:40.730 --> 00:07:44.420
A star is a huge nuclear fusion reactor.

00:07:44.450 --> 00:07:50.300
The force of its gravity is so powerful that
it fuses atoms together to make progressively

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:52.430
heavier and heavier elements.

00:07:53.880 --> 00:07:58.920
The star fuses hydrogen into helium once it
exhausts its hydrogen.

00:07:58.950 --> 00:08:02.640
Then if it's massive enough, it can start
fusing helium at its core.

00:08:04.910 --> 00:08:12.560
Fusion continues forming carbon, oxygen,
nitrogen all the way up to iron.

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:19.610
Once the star has iron in the core, it's
almost like you've poisoned it because this

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:22.890
extinguishes the nuclear reactions in the
core of the star.

00:08:22.910 --> 00:08:26.240
You fuse something into iron and you get no
energy.

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:30.320
All of a sudden, there's nothing to support
the crush of gravity.

00:08:30.350 --> 00:08:31.550
No radiation pressure.

00:08:31.550 --> 00:08:36.050
Pushing out means no pressure keeping the
outer regions from falling in.

00:08:36.050 --> 00:08:37.280
And that's what they do.

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:44.880
As the star collapses in its death throes,
its core becomes the wildest, craziest and

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:46.830
freakiest pressure cooker in the whole
universe.

00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:53.280
The ingredients are all in place.

00:08:53.310 --> 00:08:56.580
Time to start cooking up a neutron star.

